## Why This Is Asked

This situation tests ability to design robust multi-tenant resource governance with BPF maps, a common source of memory leaks in real-world deployments.

## Key Concepts

- BPF map accounting per tenant
- Map lifecycle and eviction strategies
- Namespace/isolation boundaries
- Observability with bpftool, perf, pidstat
- Canary rollouts and rollback plans

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo: enforce per-tenant map limit
struct tenant {
  __u32 id;
  __u64 limit; // bytes
  __u64 used;
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle bursts exceeding quotas without impacting others?
- What metrics and alerts would you implement?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design tenant isolation, per-tenant caching, and robust observability in a shared-kernel environment under bursty load.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant cgroup v2 controls: io.max, memory.max, pids.max
- Per-tenant namespaces and isolated build caches
- BPF/eBPF tracing for vfs, dentry, inode, and I/O paths
- Observability: Prometheus/Grafana, metrics for latency, stalls, cache hits
- Test/rollback: canary tenants, staged rollout, revert quotas

